WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.009
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Hey, Averi.

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:03.051
AVERI KITSCH: Hey, Grant.

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:04.230
What's up?

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.390
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Hey,
you would not guess this.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.650
Our critter watch app--

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.380
the traffic has been exploding
in the last few months

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.010
with all the viewers checking
out our cute animals.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.870
And our product manager, Martin?

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.740
He's requested we introduce
some realtime functionality

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.630
in our Cloud Run web app.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.140
Specifically, we're looking
to add realtime chat.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.147
AVERI KITSCH: Wow, that
sounds really cool.

00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:29.730
GRANT TIMMERMAN:
However, I'm not sure

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.369
where to start from the
technical side of things.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:39.150
I mean, there's HTTP Push, gRBC,
Websockets, server-sent events,

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.190
polling, et cetera.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.450
AVERI KITSCH: Oh, I can help.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.530
Let's walk through our options.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.640
When it comes to realtime
communication with services,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.380
there are a couple options--

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.330
server-sent events,
Websockets, and gRPC.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.930
Server-sent events
use the HTTP protocol

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.320
to create a persistent,
one-directional connection

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.590
to send data from the
server to the client.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.650
Server-sent events are great
for apps where you don't need

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.670
to send any data to the server.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.630
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh,
that sounds really good,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.530
especially for like a
Twitter-style news feed,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.070
or maybe like a realtime
dashboard of stock quotes.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.920
But in this case,
we need a chat app

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.860
to have bidirectional
communication.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.030
What are the other options?

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.850
AVERI KITSCH: Well,
Cloud Run also

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.280
supports Websockets,
which allows

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.660
us to open a two-directional
connection between the client

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.950
and server.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.860
Both the client and server
can trigger communication

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.340
with one another, and both can
send messages at the same time.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.970
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh,
that sounds amazing.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.980
AVERI KITSCH: But
wait, there is more.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.390
You also mentioned gRPC,
which can also enable

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.460
bidirectional communication.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.650
But it uses HTTP/2 protocol
and a remote procedure call

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.390
framework.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.560
Though there is a web proxy for
browser-server communication,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.650
gRPC is mostly used
for communication

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.210
between backend services.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.170
So in this case, for
a two-way chat app,

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.870
I would recommend Websockets.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.570
GRANT TIMMERMAN: OK, that
all sounds really clear.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.470
Thanks.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.790
So let's go and
look at the code.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.360
So it looks like in
Martin's chat app,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.840
we use Websockets
using Socket.IO.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.540
So how does this work?

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.810
AVERI KITSCH: Socket.IO is a
nice wrapper for Websockets

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.660
and provides some
additional features.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.230
And just like using the
Websocket API directly,

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:56.550
we will install and initialize
Socket.IO on both the client

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.670
and the server.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.150
The server will listen for
the connection event, which

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.780
allows us to access the socket
instance on both the client

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.070
and server.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:11.555
The socket instance can emit
and listen for messages.

00:03:11.555 --> 00:03:12.930
GRANT TIMMERMAN:
All right, cool.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:17.050
So how do I then deploy
this on Cloud Run?

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.420
AVERI KITSCH: One of the
great parts about Websockets

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:24.000
is it comes out of the
box with Cloud Run.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.650
I can deploy this straight from
my terminal using gcloud, which

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.940
can also handle containerizing
the application by specifying

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.950
my source directory.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.350
Now that it's deployed,
let's test it out

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.840
by logging into a chat room.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.400
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, awesome.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.930
And it looks like Martin's
already in the app.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.740
Oh, however, there
seems to be an issue.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.890
Messages are disappearing
between sessions.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.906
Do you know how to
fix that, Averi?

00:03:57.906 --> 00:04:00.530
AVERI KITSCH: I think I
know what the problem is.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.670
Cloud Run automatically scales
container instances and load

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.720
balances every request between
the available instances.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.240
Therefore, your client
may be connecting

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.190
to a different
container instance,

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:17.480
and we haven't setup any way
to share or store the data.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.540
GRANT TIMMERMAN: So I guess
we need to save the chat

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.240
messages to a database?

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.760
AVERI KITSCH: Well, a
database doesn't quite

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.400
solve this problem,
because you need to poll

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.230
the database for updates.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:32.780
But we can avoid
polling altogether

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.590
by using a Publisher/Subscriber
broker like Redis.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.800
Container instances can
publish to a specific channel,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.920
as well as subscribe
for push updates.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.620
GRANT TIMMERMAN: OK, so
yeah, that sounds good.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:50.100
How then do we add that?

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.600
AVERI KITSCH: First, we'll go
create a Redis instance for us

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.480
to use via Cloud Memorystore.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.840
Our Redis instance lives
in a virtual private cloud,

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.680
which means it's not accessible
from the outside internet.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.190
So I'll also have to go
create a serverless VPC access

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:08.800
connector, so our
Cloud Run service can

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.380
connect to our Redis instance.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.090
When we redeploy, we'll need
our Redis IP and the name

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.910
of our serverless
VPC access connector.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.265
Can you update the app
to subscribe and publish

00:05:21.265 --> 00:05:23.530
to the Redis channels?

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.650
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Well, using
Socket.IO, all I had to do

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.650
was update it to use
the Redis adapter.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:33.910
And it handles broadcasting
events to all clients,

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.610
regardless of what
container instances

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.920
they are connected to.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.290
We can connect to
the Redis instance

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:45.130
with the IP and port
environment variables

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:48.897
when deploying our
Cloud Run service.

00:05:48.897 --> 00:05:50.480
AVERI KITSCH: Let's
deploy our changes

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.670
and specify our new
Redis environment

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.590
variable and the VPC connector.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.750
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, it works.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.610
That was easy.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.823
AVERI KITSCH: I'm so
glad we got it working.

00:06:02.823 --> 00:06:04.240
GRANT TIMMERMAN:
I'm so glad, too.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.780
And now I know the difference
between server-side events,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.750
Websockets, and gRPC.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.300
It's so easy to use
Websockets with Cloud Run

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.290
to add realtime chat
to Martin's app.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:20.430
AVERI KITSCH: And don't forget,
with Websockets and Redis,

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.380
we're able to store
and coordinate

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.890
our data with thousands of users
across all of our container

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:27.300
instances.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.730
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Well,
thanks so much, Averi.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.910
I'm sure Martin
will be very happy.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:38.550
Oh, it looks like I have a
lot of messages to reply to.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.573
Yeah, let me get to that.

00:06:40.573 --> 00:06:41.490
AVERI KITSCH: Awesome.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.608
See you next time.

00:06:42.608 --> 00:06:43.650
GRANT TIMMERMAN: See you.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:47.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

